    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d19">

                <pb facs="0114" n="30" sameAs="frus:frus1886/pg30"/>

                <head>No. 19.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Tree to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-05-05"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 5, 1886</date>.
                        (Received May 17.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 88.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that I have
                    to-day transmitted to you, under separate cover, as printed matter, two copies
                    each of numbers 3 and 4 (1886) of the “Bulletin Offciel de l’État Indépendent
                        <pb facs="0115" n="31" xml:id="pg31"/> du Congo.” They contain a number of
                    decrees and ordinances with reference to the Government of the State, the most
                    important of which seems to be the decree found in No. 3, at page 46 <hi
                        rendition="#italic">et seq.</hi>, concerning the extradition of
                    criminals.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>LAMBERT TREE.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d19-a1.xml" xpointer="d19-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
